{"reference":"2 Corinthians 2","book":"2 Corinthians","chapter":2,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter moves from a painful conflict toward restoration, then from Paul’s unsettled departure from Troas into the strong imagery of triumph, fragrance, and sincere speech. Paul says his earlier letter came through anguish and tears rather than a wish to cause grief; yet the passage also leaves room to assess the effects of such communication rather than treating stated loving intent as sufficient by itself.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:1-4","2 Corinthians 2:14-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_24fa73d8389b129fcfe14a25"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Discipline and Restoration","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The unnamed person in verses 5–11 should not be confidently identified with a person or incident elsewhere. What the passage does say is that a punishment imposed by the majority was now sufficient, and that forgiveness, comfort, and reaffirmed love were needed so excessive grief would not overwhelm the person. Restoration of a person’s standing, however, does not automatically settle separate questions about trust, role, contact, or access.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:5-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_aa3a6060a7eab23ff031f00b","reader_path_2_corinthians_002_e7eedd9c2f07163edbdd577d"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Triumph, Fragrance, and Sincerity","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Paul’s language changes abruptly from anxiety over not finding Titus to thanksgiving that God leads the mission team in triumph. The triumph image draws on victory-procession imagery, but its precise role remains disputed: Paul and his coworkers may be pictured as participants in victory, as captives on display, or in a deliberately paradoxical combination. The fragrance metaphor likewise describes divergent reception of the same message—life for some and death for others—while Paul’s question, “Who is sufficient?” leads into his contrast between sincere speech before God and peddling God’s word.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:14","2 Corinthians 2:15-16","2 Corinthians 2:16-17"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_2ce524896103077986068423","reader_path_2_corinthians_002_44f099d3d519b8e98fbd9fca"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"why_it_matters","title":"Why This Matters","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The chapter’s claims about forgiveness and spiritual speech should not be used to erase accountability. Its movement from sufficient discipline toward comfort and reaffirmed love supports proportionate, reviewable action rather than permanent ostracism; similarly, the contrast with peddling God’s word subjects spiritual messaging, financial interest, and self-presentation to scrutiny rather than assumed purity.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:1-4","2 Corinthians 2:14-17","2 Corinthians 2:6-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_a448fdd9f0e27c5246a33900"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_5","text":"The aroma image concerns contrasting reception of the message, not the worth or contamination of particular kinds of bodies or peoples. Likewise, the appeal to avoid Satan’s advantage in the forgiveness discussion cannot justify blaming survivors, denying care, or treating disagreement as demonic possession.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 2:10-11","2 Corinthians 2:14-16","2 Corinthians 2:5-11"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_9331f0032a82aa589fc83863"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Wider Letter Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"The opening continues Paul’s earlier defense of changing his travel plans: he presents the avoided visit as an attempt to spare the Corinthians, not as fickleness. The wider itinerary remains difficult to reconstruct with certainty from the available accounts.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 1:12-24","2 Corinthians 1:15-24"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_5cc6db32e2056d12dd2bcd78","reader_path_2_corinthians_002_77f6c33297b98c8c1ec0e859"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_7","text":"For the wider letter, Papyrus 46 preserves most of 2 Corinthians, Papyrus 99 preserves portions, and major codices provide further witnesses with distinct textual profiles.","verse_refs":["2 Corinthians 10:1-18","2 Corinthians 11:1-33","2 Corinthians 12:1-21","2 Corinthians 13:1-14","2 Corinthians 1:1-24","2 Corinthians 3:1-18","2 Corinthians 4:1-18","2 Corinthians 5:1-21","2 Corinthians 6:1-18","2 Corinthians 7:1-16","2 Corinthians 8:1-24","2 Corinthians 9:1-15"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_2_corinthians_002_a4b72a9aa4baae42b3924799"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}